  "contract": "Hydration boundary: the value produced where there is no live store must equal the first client render's value and be referentially stable. Only react-redux names getServerSnapshot; zustand expresses it through useSyncExternalStoreWithSelector, primitives through an is-hydrated flag, router through its hydrate wrapper, query through its dehydrate/hydrate pair, db through its live-query hook - six different vocabularies for one contract.",
  "defect_location": null,
  "case_id": "xrepo-v7-2-first-paint-contradicts-what-was-sent-hard",
  "case_question": "Something rendered away from the browser arrives already filled in, and for a single frame after it becomes interactive it is replaced by an empty or default version of itself before settling back to what it first showed. On a fast connection it reads as a flicker; on a slow one people report the real content 'arriving twice'. In at least one of these libraries the same situation instead produces a hard complaint in the console about the two renderings not agreeing, and a subtree that silently stops being interactive. The libraries here differ in what they hand back when asked for a value in a setting where there is no live source to read from, and in whether that answer is guaranteed to be the same one the first interactive render will compute. Which files decide what is handed back in that setting, and which own the requirement that the two renderings agree?",
